`,(0,n.jsx)(i,{type:"tip",title:"What to put in your policy",children:(0,n.jsx)(r.p,{children:"Your policy should ask staff to leave out names and identifiers where they can, and require that a person reviews anything before it goes out."})}),`
`,(0,n.jsx)(r.h2,{id:"phase-2---write-your-ai-use-policy",children:"Phase 2 - Write your AI use policy"}),`
`,(0,n.jsx)(r.p,{children:"If you don't have a written AI policy yet, that's normal. Write something short first and treat it as a living document. You're not starting from scratch: as Phase 1 said, your existing confidentiality rules still apply, and this policy mostly says how they carry over to Claude."}),`
`,(0,n.jsx)(r.p,{children:"Don't try to cover every team and every scenario in one pass. Start where the need is clearest (one program team, one type of data, one connector) and add to it as staff bring you new use cases and as Claude changes. Holding out for one comprehensive policy is how organizations stall, and the next product update would reopen it anyway."}),`
`,(0,n.jsx)(r.p,{children:"Step 1 asks staff before you draft anything, so the policy answers their real questions in their own words."}),`
